# Source registry — gap-keyword → authoritative source (read by scripts.foundry.scrapers.WebSourceScout
# and by the worker's default foundry). One JSON object per line; '#' lines are comments.
#
# How matching works: WebSourceScout.find() lowercases (gap.summary + source_hints + industry) and
# matches an entry if ANY of its `keywords` is a substring. So key on the gov domain and/or industry
# from data/research-queue/areas.jsonl. Live scrape needs network; offline the fetch fails closed.
#
# `gov: true` ⇒ publisher_class=public_authority ⇒ openness.classify = OPEN content; a `freshness:volatile`
# corpus is then billed as live_subscription delivery by access.py (the maintained feed is the product).
#
# NOTE on parsing: the default parse_facts() strips tags + splits on blank lines — good for HTML/text
# pages. JSON APIs (eCFR versioner) parse as one blob and want a per-source parser (see the capstone's
# custom json parse); they're registered here as verified-fetchable targets, flagged `"format": "json"`.
